1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,200
What does it take to build a bank's e-commerce acquiring system from scratch?

2
00:00:04,250 --> 00:00:09,576
This series walks through one, part by part, and this map shows where each part sits.

3
00:00:09,626 --> 00:00:18,792
On top are the three users: the merchant's server, the buyer, and the bank admin; inside is one modular monolith with six modules.

4
00:00:18,842 --> 00:00:24,696
Partner owns the merchants: who they are, their company documents, and whether they may sell.

5
00:00:24,746 --> 00:00:27,840
Commission decides the fee on every payment.

6
00:00:27,890 --> 00:00:35,064
The e POS module knows which e POS terminal, a MID and a TID, each payment goes through.

7
00:00:35,114 --> 00:00:40,512
The BIN module knows which scheme and which bank issued a card, from its first digits.

8
00:00:40,562 --> 00:00:44,808
The card vault keeps card numbers encrypted, and hands out tokens instead.

9
00:00:44,858 --> 00:00:49,248
Payment creates every payment, debits the card, and resolves the outcome.

10
00:00:49,298 --> 00:00:57,168
Below the app, separate services do one job each: email, and an adapter for each card scheme, Uzcard and Humo.

11
00:00:57,218 --> 00:01:07,704
The shared infrastructure is Postgres, Redis, Keycloak, Vault, an S3 file store, Temporal, Kafka, and monitoring with Prometheus and Grafana.

12
00:01:07,754 --> 00:01:22,584
Part zero brings up the whole infrastructure with one command: Postgres and Redis, Mailpit, the S3 store, Keycloak, the processing sandbox, Vault, monitoring, Temporal and Kafka, each explained in the episode that first uses it.

13
00:01:22,634 --> 00:01:31,680
Part one covers the merchants: sign-up, email codes, company documents, two-admin approval, and logins and API clients in Keycloak.

14
00:01:31,730 --> 00:01:37,728
Part two holds the money rules: the commission, and the e POS terminals every debit goes through.

15
00:01:37,778 --> 00:01:47,232
Part three handles cards: the Uzcard and Humo adapters as their own services, the BIN module with its bank directory, and a vault that stores only tokens.

16
00:01:47,282 --> 00:01:55,080
Part four is the payment, last, with monitoring, durable retries, and Kafka events, because it needs everything else.

17
00:01:55,130 --> 00:02:03,000
Part five deploys everything to Kubernetes, then moves the card vault into its own service, to show why clean boundaries matter.

18
00:02:03,050 --> 00:02:07,440
Next, the project setup, which brings the whole world up with one command.
